I want to make some modifications in the 'PUT_ClearContract' condition.
The original condition is [arrList.Count == 0 && arrList.Capacity == 0].

Could you please suggest a way to modify it to make an assertion like this "Debug.Assert(count = 0 && capacity = 0)"?


A: Assuming you have a code like this:
int count = arrList.Count;
int capacity = arrList.Capacity;
Debug.Assert(count == 0 && capacity == 0);

The condition is already correct and it asserts whether count is 0 and capacity is 0. If both condition are true, it passes the assert. If either of them is false, it fails the assert.
So, you do not need to make any changes.
Also make sure that you are running this test with the correct settings. You must include this test in your test suite and it should be run using the NUnit UI (Test Runner) or by command line if you use NUnit Console Runner.
Make sure you have set the `Debug.AssertIng enabled`  from Debug -> Options -> Debugging -> Enable property evaluation and Assert if option in Visual Studio.
You can get more on how to use Debug.Assert here: https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.diagnostics.debug.assert?view=net-5.0
Good luck with your programming, it's an interesting field to be in.

Also you may want to revise your comments for better understanding. Commenting your assertions would be good practice. Here is an example:

// Assert that count is 0 and capacity is 0
Debug.Assert(count == 0 && capacity == 0);

This will help you to know for whom this assert is failing.

A: In C#, the == operator is used for equality comparison. It will return true if both sides are equal, and false otherwise.
Instead of changing code, you can directly assign the statement like this:
Debug.Assert(count == 0 && capacity == 0);

The above line will throw an exception if "count" and "capacity" are not both 0, proving that your ArrayList has been cleared.
Additionally, you might want to check if your assertions are being executed in Debug mode only by adding a conditional compilation symbol DEBUG. This is helpful when running tests in non-debug mode without throwing exceptions. Here's how you can do it:
#if DEBUG
   Debug.Assert(count == 0 && capacity == 0);
#endif

The above line of code will only get executed in Debug build of your project, and will not impact the performance of your Release build.
